title: Comrade Thomas
subtitle: The International's man in Berlin has money to offer.
new-page: true
tags: event
view-if: year = 1920 and month = 5 and crisis = 0 and ending_id = 0
max-visits: 1

= Comrade Thomas

The Communist International keeps a West European Secretariat in Berlin. Its head calls himself Comrade Thomas. He runs a publishing house and a network of couriers, and he has a great deal of money, some of it in jewels sold off from the Tsar's treasury.

The party is short of paper, presses and organisers' wages, and the election is a month away. Thomas says Moscow would be glad to help every month.

- @subsidy: Accept a monthly subsidy.
- @once: Take money for the election, and nothing more.
- @refuse: Refuse, and live on the members' dues.

@subsidy
subtitle: One more fund every month. Every district secretary will know who pays him.
on-arrival: {!
Q.moscow_subsidy += 1;
Q.independence -= 10;
Q.comintern_rep = 'Comrade Thomas';
RG.ecci('zinoviev', 8);
!}

The money arrives on the first of every month through a bank in Amsterdam.

@once
subtitle: 3 funds now.
on-arrival: resources += 3; independence -= 3

Thomas hands over a sealed envelope and a receipt to sign. The posters are printed the following week.

@refuse
subtitle: The party stays poor, and its own.
on-arrival: {!
Q.independence += 8;
RG.ecci('zinoviev', -8);
!}

Thomas puts his envelope back in his coat. His report to Zinoviev says the German Zentrale has delusions of independence.
